Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 12   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Cubics, Hessians, and Inflections",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["8 November 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Cubics", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Finding and classifying cubics", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Nodal and cuspidal cubics", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
One key to the classification of cubic curves is a counting of \
double points.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A non-degenerate cubic has ",
  StyleBox["at most one double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "A line intersects a nondegenerate cubic 3 times.  If there are 2 double \
points, the line connecting them intersects the cubic at least 4 times.  This \
is impossible when the line is not a ",
  StyleBox["factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A non-degenerate quartic has ",
  StyleBox["at most three double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "A conic intersects a nondegenerate quartic 8 times.  If there are 4 double \
points, the conic passing through them and through any further point of the \
curve intersects the quartic at least 9 times.  This is impossible.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A  non-degenerate curve of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can have at most  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n - 1)\)\ \((n - 2)\)\),
        FontSize->11,
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " double points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Suppose not.  Consider the curve of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n - 2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " determined by the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n - 1)\)\ \((n - 2)\) + 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " double points and an appropriate number of additional points of the given \
curve.  There are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n + 1)\)\ \((n + 2)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " monomials of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Therefore, a curve of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is determined by  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(1\/2\ \((n + 1)\)\ \((n + 2)\) - 1 == 1\/2\ n\ \((n + 3)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "points, and a curve of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n - 2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is determined by",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n - 2)\)\ \((n + 1)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " points. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If there are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n - 1)\)\ \((n - 2)\) + 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " double points on an ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "th-degree curve, the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\((n - 2)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "nd-degree curve passing through them and through further \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(1\/2\ \((n - 2)\)\ \((n + 1)\) - 1\/2\ \((n - 1)\)\ \((n - 2)\) - 
            1 == n - 3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \npoints of the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-curve intersects it at least \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(2\ \((1\/2\ \((n - 1)\)\ \((n - 2)\) + 1)\) + \((n - 3)\) == 
          n\^2 - 2\ n + 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\ntimes.  But an ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\((n - 2)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "nd-degree curve intersects an ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "th-degree curve only ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n\ \((n - 2)\) == n\^2 - 2\ n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "times.  We have a contradiction.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["non-degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (",
  StyleBox["i.e.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it does not factor into lower degree polynomials).  Then the multiple \
points of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["cannot be worse than double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "   Because, if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " had a ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it would factor into linear factors.  Moreover, as we argued, a \
non-degenerate cubic cannot have ",
  StyleBox["two double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We note that the following three classes of non-degenerate cubics are \
projectively invariant; however, this does not give a ",
  StyleBox["complete",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " classification."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A cubic ",
  StyleBox["without a double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is called ",
  StyleBox["non singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  \nA cubic with a double point which has ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is called ",
  StyleBox["nodal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \nA cubic with a double point with a ",
  StyleBox["double tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is called ",
  StyleBox["cuspidal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["Here are some pictures.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The well known singular curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      AdjustmentBox[
        StyleBox[\(y\^2 == x\^3\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"],
        BoxMargins->{{0.0463768, -0.0463768}, {0, 0}}]]],
  " has many interesting \"perturbations\".  Here is one."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, e]; \n
    f = Tidy[y\^2\ z - x\ \((x - z)\)\ \((x - e\ z)\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] 2}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 6}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \n\tPlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.007]}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That was ",
  StyleBox["non singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Try some other values of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Particular values of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " do give singular curves."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 131, \n\t
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \n\tPlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.007]}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f /. e \[Rule] 1, \((u + w)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That was ",
  StyleBox["nodal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  And we can see that it has an inflection point at infinity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. {z \[Rule] y, y \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 7}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \n\tPlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.007]}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
But, all the curves of this family have inflection points \
there.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(PD[f, v]\), 
    \(PD[f, v\^2]\), 
    \(PD[f, v\^3]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Degererate cubics", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We are going to prove eventually that all non-degenerate nodal cubics ",
  StyleBox["are",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projectively equivalent; that all non-degenerate cuspidal cubics ",
  StyleBox["are",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projectively equivalent; but that there are infinitely many non-singular \
cubics that ",
  StyleBox["are",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projectively equivalent.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, in the last case there will be a computational test for \
projective equivalence.  It will also transpire that this classification \
makes very heavy use of ",
  StyleBox["complex numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] in order to obtain suitable transformations \[LongDash] while \
the classification over the real numbers is much harder to carry out and will \
not be attempted in this course.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
As regards degenerate cubics, here is what we can say, just by \
counting possibilities. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "There are ",
  StyleBox["six",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " types of ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " cubics:\n\t",
  StyleBox["(1)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " a triple line (",
  StyleBox["i.e.,",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the same line counted three times);\n\t",
  StyleBox["(2)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " a line and a distinct double line;\n\t",
  StyleBox["(3) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "three distinct concurrent lines;\n\t",
  StyleBox["(4)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " three distinct non-concurrent lines;\n\t",
  StyleBox["(5)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " a non-degenerate conic and a tangent;\n\t",
  StyleBox["(6)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " a non-degenerate conic and a secant."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is clear that these are all the cases and that they are ",
  StyleBox["projective invariants",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (This means that if a curve lies in one of the classes, then so does \
every image of the curve under a projective transformation.)  Also \
\[LongDash] from what we know about lines and conics \[LongDash] any two \
curves of one class are equivalent.  But, a curve of one class ",
  StyleBox["is not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " equivalent to one of a ",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " class.  Thus, we have a full projective classification in the degenerate \
case."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A good question is whether there a simple ",
  StyleBox["test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for degeneracy.  We do not have a completely satisfactory answer.  Of \
course, degenerate cubics have ",
  StyleBox["multiple points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We can always search for them first.  (How?)  We note additionally \
that, since the Hessian matrix consists of ",
  StyleBox["second dertivatives",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a polynomial, in the case of a cubic these entries will all be ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Therefore, the ",
  StyleBox["Hessian determinant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of a cubic polynomial is ",
  StyleBox["again a cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Checking the Hessian is also a way to detect degeneracy.  Let us try \
out some examples."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Consider first a degenerate cubic ",
  StyleBox["h ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "consisting of ",
  StyleBox["three lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We can work symbolically. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[g, h, Subscript]; \ng\_1 = a\_1\ x + b\_1\ y + c\_1\ z; \n
    g\_2 = a\_2\ x + b\_2\ y + c\_2\ z; \ng\_3 = a\_3\ x + b\_3\ y + c\_3\ z; 
    \nh = g\_1\ g\_2\ g\_3; \nDegP[h]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Let us investigate the Hessian determinant.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Det[MHP[h]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Amazingly it factors into the ",
  StyleBox["original three lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " themselves times a squared scalar factor that is the determinant of the \
coordinates of the three lines."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[g\_1, PE[g\_2, g\_3]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, if the three lines are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " concurrent, the Hessian is ",
  StyleBox["non zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In the case of one or two lines, however, it will have to be zero.  The \
vanishing of the Hessian is of course a bad sign!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Consider next the case of a ",
  StyleBox["non-degererate conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and a ",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We can asume the conic is a special one."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, f, g, h]; \n\(f = x\^2 + y\^2 + z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(g = a\ x + b\ y + c\ z; \)\), 
    \(\(h = f\ g; \)\), 
    \(DegP[h]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["We factor the Hessian determinant.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Det[MHP[h]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The line is again a factor.  So one test for degeneracy is to check \
whether the curve and its Hessian have a ",
  StyleBox["common factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   Next, we look at the quadratic part.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(k = Tidy[Last[%]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["That is a nice conic, and we can ask when it is degenerate.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Det[MHP[k]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That looks familiar!  Here is the envelope of the original conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Envel[f]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[k]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " vanishes if, and only if, the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  In that case, the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["k",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " might simplify."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[1\/4\ PolynomialMod[k, a\^2 + b\^2 + c\^2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This does not seem an improvement, but remember ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\^2 + b\^2 + c\^2 == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This means the polynomial can be written as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[
      a\^2\ x\^2 + 2\ a\ b\ x\ y + b\^2\ y\^2 + 2\ a\ c\ x\ z + 
        2\ b\ c\ y\ z + c\^2\ z\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["And it factors!  ", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Perhaps we have not given a very straight-foward \"test\" for degeneracy, \
but ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can help with the algebra in finding these common factors.  For example, \
we remark that the command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["PolynomialGCD",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " applied to a list of polynomials gives the greatest common divisor of the \
given polynomials. But note that in using this command, all symbolic \
parameters are treated as ",
  StyleBox["variables",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "; moreover, it will by default treat ",
  StyleBox["algebraic numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that appear in the polynomials also as independent variables.   One has \
to write especially an option for the extension field in the form\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Extension \[Rule] Automatic\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nso that the call extends the coefficient field to include algebraic \
numbers that appear in the polynomials.  For simple examples it is probably \
best to restrict attention to polynomials with ",
  StyleBox["rational ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(or ",
  StyleBox["integer",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") coefficients."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Cubics passing through nine points", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Just as we found a formula for the conic through ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points, we can write a similar formula for a cubic through ",
  StyleBox["nine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points.  In the conic case we had only to be sure that no ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points were collinear to be able to get a non-degenerarte conic.  In the \
cubic case the situation is more subtle, as we shall see."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What we do is to take the coordinates for ",
  StyleBox["nine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points with coordinates ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a\_i, b\_i, c\_i}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(i == 1,  ... , 9\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and put then into the followin ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(10\[Times]10\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "matrix."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
              {\(x\^3\), \(x\^2\ y\), \(x\ y\^2\), \(y\^3\), \(x\^2\ z\), 
                \(x\ y\ z\), \(y\^2\ z\), \(x\ z\^2\), \(y\ z\^2\), 
                \(z\^3\)},
              {\(a\_1\%3\), \(a\_1\%2\ b\_1\), \(a\_1\ b\_1\%2\), 
                \(b\_1\%3\), \(a\_1\%2\ c\_1\), \(a\_1\ b\_1\ c\_1\), 
                \(b\_1\%2\ c\_1\), \(a\_1\ c\_1\%2\), \(b\_1\ c\_1\%2\), 
                \(c\_1\%3\)},
              {\(a\_2\%3\), \(a\_2\%2\ b\_2\), \(a\_2\ b\_2\%2\), 
                \(b\_2\%3\), \(a\_2\%2\ c\_2\), \(a\_2\ b\_2\ c\_2\), 
                \(b\_2\%2\ c\_2\), \(a\_2\ c\_2\%2\), \(b\_2\ c\_2\%2\), 
                \(c\_2\%3\)},
              {\(a\_3\%3\), \(a\_3\%2\ b\_3\), \(a\_3\ b\_3\%2\), 
                \(b\_3\%3\), \(a\_3\%2\ c\_3\), \(a\_3\ b\_3\ c\_3\), 
                \(b\_3\%2\ c\_3\), \(a\_3\ c\_3\%2\), \(b\_3\ c\_3\%2\), 
                \(c\_3\%3\)},
              {\(a\_4\%3\), \(a\_4\%2\ b\_4\), \(a\_4\ b\_4\%2\), 
                \(b\_4\%3\), \(a\_4\%2\ c\_4\), \(a\_4\ b\_4\ c\_4\), 
                \(b\_4\%2\ c\_4\), \(a\_4\ c\_4\%2\), \(b\_4\ c\_4\%2\), 
                \(c\_4\%3\)},
              {\(a\_5\%3\), \(a\_5\%2\ b\_5\), \(a\_5\ b\_5\%2\), 
                \(b\_5\%3\), \(a\_5\%2\ c\_5\), \(a\_5\ b\_5\ c\_5\), 
                \(b\_5\%2\ c\_5\), \(a\_5\ c\_5\%2\), \(b\_5\ c\_5\%2\), 
                \(c\_5\%3\)},
              {\(a\_6\%3\), \(a\_6\%2\ b\_6\), \(a\_6\ b\_6\%2\), 
                \(b\_6\%3\), \(a\_6\%2\ c\_6\), \(a\_6\ b\_6\ c\_6\), 
                \(b\_6\%2\ c\_6\), \(a\_6\ c\_6\%2\), \(b\_6\ c\_6\%2\), 
                \(c\_6\%3\)},
              {\(a\_7\%3\), \(a\_7\%2\ b\_7\), \(a\_7\ b\_7\%2\), 
                \(b\_7\%3\), \(a\_7\%2\ c\_7\), \(a\_7\ b\_7\ c\_7\), 
                \(b\_7\%2\ c\_7\), \(a\_7\ c\_7\%2\), \(b\_7\ c\_7\%2\), 
                \(c\_7\%3\)},
              {\(a\_8\%3\), \(a\_8\%2\ b\_8\), \(a\_8\ b\_8\%2\), 
                \(b\_8\%3\), \(a\_8\%2\ c\_8\), \(a\_8\ b\_8\ c\_8\), 
                \(b\_8\%2\ c\_8\), \(a\_8\ c\_8\%2\), \(b\_8\ c\_8\%2\), 
                \(c\_8\%3\)},
              {\(a\_9\%3\), \(a\_9\%2\ b\_9\), \(a\_9\ b\_9\%2\), 
                \(b\_9\%3\), \(a\_9\%2\ c\_9\), \(a\_9\ b\_9\ c\_9\), 
                \(b\_9\%2\ c\_9\), \(a\_9\ c\_9\%2\), \(b\_9\ c\_9\%2\), 
                \(c\_9\%3\)}
              }], ")"}],
        FontSize->14,
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is seen that the top row is left ",
  StyleBox["symbolic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The ",
  StyleBox["determinant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of this matrix is a ",
  StyleBox["cubic polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " which is ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " for each of the given nine points.  If the points are  ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent, then the cubic is uniquely determined (up to a scalar \
factor).  Moreover, the ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independence of the nine points means (in a way similar to our analysis \
of conics) that the lower  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(9\[Times]10\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "matrix has the ",
  StyleBox["maximal rank",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["9",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, the polynomial obtained will be ",
  StyleBox["non trivial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " when the points are ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here follow some examples, where this process of setting up the \
determinant has been coded up as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P3P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in the notebook ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Implementations . nb\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Just throwing out some points probably makes a degenerate cubic.  Note \
that three of the points are taken at infinity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(pts = {u, v, w, u + v, v + w, u + w, u + v + w, \n
        \t\t\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ u + 2\ v + w, 2\ u + 4\ v + w}; \n
    f = Factor[P3P@@pts]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The first try ",
  StyleBox["factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!  Here is the picture, first of the points chosen, then of the  cubic \
passing through them."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(cc = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-1\), 5}, {y, \(-3\), 5}, \n\t\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
        Axes \[Rule] None, \n\t\tDisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n
    \(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{cc, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Another try again ",
  StyleBox["factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pts = {u, v, w, u + v, v + w, u + w, u + v + w, 2\ u + 2\ v + w, 
        2\ u + 4\ v + w}; \)\), 
    \(f = Factor[P3P@@pts]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(cc = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-1\), 5}, {y, \(-3\), 5}, \n\t\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 70, 
        Axes \[Rule] None, \n\t\tDisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n
    \(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{cc, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 3.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  But, finally, a real cubic is found."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pts = {u, v, w, u + v, v + w, u + w, u + v + w, 2\ u + 3\ v + w, 
        2\ u + 4\ v + w}; \)\), 
    \(f = Factor[P3P@@pts]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(cc = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-1\), 7}, {y, \(-3\), 5}, \n\t\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 70, 
        Axes \[Rule] None, \n\t\tDisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n
    \(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{cc, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 4.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "And, here is another."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pts = {u, v, w, u + v, v + w, u + w, u + v + w, 4\ u - 2\ v + w, 
        3\ u + 5\ v + w}; \)\), 
    \(f = Factor[P3P@@pts]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(cc = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-1\), 6}, {y, \(-3\), 6}, \n\t\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 70, 
        Axes \[Rule] None, \n\t\tDisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n
    \(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{cc, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 5.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A small change makes a quite different curve, however."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pts = {u, v, w, u + v, v + w, u + w, u + v + w, 4\ u - v + w, 
        3\ u + 5\ v + w}; \)\), 
    \(f = Factor[P3P@@pts]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(cc = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-1\), 6}, {y, \(-3\), 6}, \n\t\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 70, 
        Axes \[Rule] None, \n\t\tDisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n
    \(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{cc, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 6.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Now, let us make all the nine points ",
  StyleBox["finite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pts = {w, u + w, 2\ u + w, v + w, 2\ v + w, u + v + w, 
        PE[PE[u + w, 2\ v + w], PE[2\ u + w, v + w]], u + 2\ v + w, 
        3\ u + v + w}; \)\), 
    \(f = Factor[P3P@@pts]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(cc = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-1\), 4}, {y, \(-3\), 4}, \n\t\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 70, 
        Axes \[Rule] None, \n\t\tDisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n
    \(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{cc, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell["This is a nice curve with three real inflection points.", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Intersections and inflections on cubics", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inflections and Hessians", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["Recall the definition of an inflection point:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A simple point (",
  StyleBox["1-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "tuple point)  on a curve is an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "if the tangent to the curve at the point has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or more."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, a tangent at a ",
  StyleBox["multiple point ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "always has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or more.  In the case of a cubic curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " if, and only if, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^3] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^2] \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "factors into two distinct linear factors.  Note that this makes the \
definition of inflection point an ",
  StyleBox["algebraic definition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " using ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["PD",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We can ",
  StyleBox["test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a simple point for being an inflection point either by seeing whether the \
conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[PD[f, p]]] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", or seeing whether the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      AdjustmentBox[
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["PD",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["[",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["f",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              SuperscriptBox[
                StyleBox["p",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["]",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
        BoxMargins->{{0.0927536, -0.0927536}, {0, 0}}]]],
  " has a ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " using the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Solve",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " command.\nNote also that if the point",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is in fact the tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", it has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and it is a factor of the degenerate conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a cubic, then the Hessian cubic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(h\  == \ Det[MHP[f]]\ \),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "passes exactly through the double point (if any) and through the \
inflection points of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Therefore, a cubic can have at most ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " inflection points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["First proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Here is a short proof of the theorem.  For a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "on the cubic curve, the condition that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " factors into linear factors means that ",
  StyleBox["either",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " it is a double point (",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on ",
  StyleBox["both",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") ",
  StyleBox["or",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that it is an inflection point (",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the intersection point).  Now that condition that a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " factors is equivalent to saying that \n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[PD[f, p]]] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  \n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A typical entry of this determinant is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, p], u\ v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", say.  But this can be rewritten as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, u\ v], p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since the derivative ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, u\ v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial when ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is cubic, the scalar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, u\ v], p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is just the ",
  StyleBox["evaluation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, u\ v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at the coordinates of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But then, by the Evaluation Theorem, it follows that \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(3!\)\ Det[MHP[PD[f, p]]] == PD[Det[MHP[f]], p\^3]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nTherefore, for a point on a cubic curve, we conclude that it lies on \
the Hessian cubic if, and only if, either it is either a double point or an \
inflection point.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Second proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Another, longer proof of the theorem can be given using ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Let us just declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be a cubic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f]; \nf /: DegP[f] = 3; \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Let us look again at the general form of the Hessian matrix and at \
the determinant:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[f]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h = Det[MHP[f]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We know from the invariance theorem that if we replace",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by another triple of independent points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the determinant of this Hessian matrix, which we have called ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", is multiplied by a numerical factor.  We want to take advantage of this \
change of basis in discussing special points on the cubic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be an inflection point.  First it is declared to be on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is declared to be on the tangent, and then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is declared to be a double point of the conic polar at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  This makes ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " an inflection point.  Then we declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be another point on the conic polar which is also on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  When ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an inflection  point \[LongDash] and thus the conic polar is ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] then these points are independent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[p, q, r]; \n\((\((# /: DegP[#] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r}; \n
    \((\((p /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^3]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2\ q]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ q]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, r\^3]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ r\^2]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now let us take the formal expression for the Hessian matrix and replace ",
  
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then we take the determinant, treat it as a curve and see whether the \
point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " belongs to the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(hes = h /. {u \[Rule] p, v \[Rule] q, w \[Rule] r}\), 
    \(PD[hes, p\^3]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Remarkably enough it does!  So we have established ",
  StyleBox["in full generality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that every inflection point of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " also lies on the Hessian cubic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (But the change of basis ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u \[Rule] p, v \[Rule] q, w \[Rule] r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " was what made the symbolic computation easy.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can also reach some other conclusions:  Let us use this mode of \
calculation to compare the tangents to the cubic and to the Hessian at the \
point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, p\^2], PD[hes, p\^2]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^2]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", but it does not lie on the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, q\^2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as remarked before (because it is not on the cubic).  Therefore, the \
Hessian and the cubic ",
  StyleBox["do not share",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the same tangent at the point of intersection.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will find that it is very interesting also to look at linear \
combinations of the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with its Hessian ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Here is the conic polar of such a combination, where, remember, we have \
calculated the Hessian relative to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_1 = PD[a\ f + b\ hes, p]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will want to look at the Hessian (scalar) determinant of this ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[MHP[f\_1]] /. {u \[Rule] p, v \[Rule] q, w \[Rule] r}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is zero.  So, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an inflection point of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us now start again and declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be an ",
  StyleBox["nodal point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  First, it is declared to be on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", as a double point, then points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are declared to be on the tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " via the conic polar which is degenerate. We think of these points as ",
  StyleBox["independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[p, q, r]; \)\), 
    \(\((\((# /: DegP[#] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r}; \n
    \((\((p /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ q\^2]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ r\^2]]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can now check that in this case ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must lie on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash]in fact it is a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[hes, p\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, let us start again and declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be a ",
  StyleBox["cusp",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  First it is declared to be on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", as a double point, then a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is declared to be another double point of the conic polar at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " which in the case of a cusp degenerates to a double line \[LongDash] the \
tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " counted twice.  Then any other point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is chosen on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We can think of these points as independent, as the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot cut the curve in any other point than ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[p, q, r]; \)\), 
    \(\((\((# /: DegP[#] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r}; \n
    \((\((p /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ q]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, r\^3]]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We check now that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is this time is a ",
  StyleBox["triple point  (!) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of ",
  StyleBox["hes",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] and so it certainly lies on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["hes",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[hes, p]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In this case the Hessian cubic degenerates to three lines at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", two of them are the tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and the other passes through the one inflection point of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Finally, in the last phase of the argument, let us declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to just to be a ",
  StyleBox["simple point ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "intersects the curve there only twice, so the tangent cuts ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "at another point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be chosen on the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We think of these points as independent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[p, q, r]; \)\), 
    \(\((\((# /: DegP[#] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r}; \n
    \((\((p /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^3]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2\ q]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^3]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^2\ r]]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[hes, p\^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not on the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not on the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  So this last quantity ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " be zero, and so ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lie on the Hessian.  The proof is (finally) finished!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Note that we have proved in the middle of this long discussion \
several other facts:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "For the non-singular cubic,  the Hessian is never tangent to the cubic, \
and so \[LongDash] provided that we can show that the Hessian of the \
non-singular cubic has ",
  StyleBox["no double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] the two curves must intersect in ",
  StyleBox["9 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In other words, the non-singular cubic has ",
  StyleBox["the full complement of inflection points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["Also:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " An inflection point of the cubic is also an inflection point of any ",
  StyleBox["linear combination",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the cubic and its Hessian."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Intersecting in nine points", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We noted that as regards the ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " inflection points, there was a pencil of cubics passing through them.  \
This is a special case of a more general situation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Given ",
  StyleBox["eight",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points that are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-independent, then all cubics passing through these points must also pass \
through a fixed ",
  StyleBox["additional point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let the eight given points be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ... , ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  As when discussing independence of points earlier, we noted that the \
linear dimension of the space of cubics passing through these points is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["2",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\( == \ 10\  - \ 8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " therefore be two ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["independent",
        "Text",
        FontWeight->"Bold",
        FontSlant->"Italic"]]],
  " cubics passing through these points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at one of the given points, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then the curves must share a common tangent at that point.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a point on this tangent different from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   Now, the ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " degree-",
  StyleBox["3 uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials\n\t\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_4\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_5\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_6\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_7\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\ p\_1\%2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nhave to be ",
  StyleBox["linearly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["dependent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because there are two independent elements in the dual space that \
annihilate these ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " polynomials.   Consider, therefore, a non-trivial linear combination:\n ",
  
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{" ", 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%3\), "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_2\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_2\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_3\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_3\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_4\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_4\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_5\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_5\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_6\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_6\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_7\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_7\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_8\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_8\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_9\), 
                StyleBox["q",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox[" ",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox[\(p\_1\%2\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "==", "0"}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nNow the coefficient ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_9\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot be zero, because the eight given points are \[LongDash] by \
assumption \[LongDash] ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-independent.  This means that any cubic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " through the eight points (",
  StyleBox["i.e.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " annihilates the eight cubes) must always have the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as a ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In other words, any two cubics passing through the eight points must \
have higher-degree contact with each other at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, the ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th point is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox["counted twice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " intersect at another point, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_9\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from all the eight given ones.  (Two cubics must intersect in nine points \
\[LongDash] some, perhaps, counted as multiple intersections.  The first case \
covered the multiple possibility.)  This means that the ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " degree-",
  StyleBox["3 uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials\n\t\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_4\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_5\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_6\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_7\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_9\%3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nmust thus be linearly ",
  StyleBox["dependent",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", because there are two independent elements in the dual space that \
annihilate these ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " polynomials.  Consider, therefore, a non-trivial linear combination:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{" ", 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%3\), "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_2\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_2\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_3\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_3\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_4\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_4\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_5\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_5\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_6\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_6\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_7\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_7\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_8\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(p\_8\%3\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", \(a\_9\ p\_9\%3\)}], "==", 
            "0"}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As before, the coefficient ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_9\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot be zero, since otherwise the given eight points would not be ",
  StyleBox["3-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "independent.  Therefore any other cubic passing through the given points \
must also pass through this ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th point.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Assuming we can prove that the Hessian of a non-singular cubic is \
non-singular (which we will do later), we will know there are ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distinct inflection points, because the two curves intersect at exactly \
those points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Any cubic passing through ",
  StyleBox["eight",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the nine inflection points of the non-singular cubic must also pass \
through the ",
  StyleBox["ninth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and it must have these nine points as ",
  StyleBox["inflection points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let the non-singular cubic be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and let its Hessian be the (non-singular) cubic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  These two curves intersect in nine points (assuming non-singularity).  \
Hence, by the previous theorem, any curve passing through eight of these \
points must pass through the other intersection point.  As ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", any curve passing through these points must be of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ f + b\ h\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and we proved earlier that all these curves share the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection points.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " We still need to prove that the Hessian of the non-singular cubic has ",
  StyleBox["no double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to know that a non-singular cubic has exactly nine distinct inflection \
points.  ",
  StyleBox["This gap has to be filled.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We will have to wait until we find a ",
  StyleBox["normal form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for non-singular cubics, since then we can compute the Hessian and see \
directly it is non-singular."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A good question is when eight points are ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent.  In an earlier chapter, we showed that a suite of eight ",
  StyleBox["distinct ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "points no ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " collinear and no ",
  StyleBox["six",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conconic is indeed ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent.  But the assumption is too strong for considering points on \
a cubic.  A line can very well intersect a cubic in three points; a conic can \
intersect a cubic in six points.  Here is a picture showing the possibility."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, l, g]; \nf = y\^2\ z - x\ \((x - z)\)\ \((x + 2\ z)\); \n
    l = x - y; \ng = 3  x\^2 + 9  y\^2 - 15  z\^2; \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, l == 0, g == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 4}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \n
      PlotStyle -> {{Thickness[0.006], Black}, {Thickness[0.006], Blue}, {
            Thickness[0.006], Red}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In this example, the ",
  StyleBox["nine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points of intersection are definitely ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent. (Why?) However, we would like to show that any subset of ",
  StyleBox["eight",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are.  So this gives nine points such that any cubic through eight must \
also pass through the ninth.  This also shows that if we take eight of these \
points and ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point on the given cubic ",
  StyleBox["different from ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the ninth, then the new set of nine points ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent.  (Why?)  Here, then, is the improved independence theorem."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Any suite of eight ",
  StyleBox["distinct ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "points with no ",
  StyleBox["four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " collinear and no ",
  StyleBox["seven",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conconic is indeed ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let the eight given points be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ... , ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Suppose they satisfy the hypothesis of the theorem but are not ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent. We therefore have a non-trivial dependence relationship:\n\t\
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%3\), "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_2\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_2\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_3\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_3\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_4\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_4\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_5\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_5\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_6\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_6\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_7\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_7\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", \(a\_8\ p\_8\%3\)}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nNot all coefficients can be zero, but, in fact, since the points are \
distinct, at least ",
  StyleBox["four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coefficients are not zero.  (Why?) However, suppose at least ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of them ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", say ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The dependence equation then looks like:\n\t   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%3\), "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_2\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_2\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_3\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_3\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_4\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_4\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_5\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_5\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{\(a\_6\), " ", 
              StyleBox[\(p\_6\%3\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", \(a\_7\ p\_7\%3\)}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nWe can reorder points to assume that the ",
  StyleBox["first four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coefficients are not zero. Take a non-zero conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (degenerate or not) passing through the ",
  StyleBox["last five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p\_3, \ p\_4, p\_5, p\_6, p\_7}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  \nBy assumption, this conic cannot pass through ",
  StyleBox["both",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?) Suppose it ",
  StyleBox["misses",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a line passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Define a ",
  StyleBox["cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g == f\ l\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If we apply the function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[g, #]&\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to both sides of the dependence equation, we will annihilate all but the \
first term.  (Why?)  We will then have:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(3!\)\ a\_1\ PD[f, p\_1\%2]\ PD[l, p\_1] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nSince none of the terms on the left is zero, this is impossible.  This \
contradiction proves that ",
  StyleBox["none",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the coefficients in the original dependence realtionship is zero.  \
That was the first step of the proof."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose next that ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the eight points are ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  By reording the points, we can assume that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["{",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], ",", \(p\_2\), ",", \(p\_3\)}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " lie on a line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If we apply the function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[l, #]&\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to both sides of the original dependence equation, we will annihilate the \
first ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " terms.  (Why?)  We will then have:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          \(3 a\_4\ PD[l, p\_4] p\_4\%2 + 3 a\_5\ PD[l, p\_4] p\_5\%2 + 
            3 a\_6\ PD[l, p\_4] p\_6\%2 + \n\t3 a\_7\ PD[l, p\_4] p\_7\%2 + 
            3 a\_8\ PD[l, p\_4] p\_8\%2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["0.",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "\nSince the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot pass through any of the other points by assumption, none of the \
coefficients is zero.  So, we have a dependence relation among the ",
  StyleBox["five squares",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This means \[LongDash] by a result we have used many times in \
connection with conics \[LongDash] that three of the points are ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Why?)  By reordering the points, we can assume that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["{",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "4"], ",", \(p\_5\), ",", \(p\_6\)}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " lie on a line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " pass through either of the other two points.     If we apply the function \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[m, #]&\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to both sides of the new dependence equation just obtained, we will get a \
non-trivial dependence relation between ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_7\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This is ",
  StyleBox["impossible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for distinct points.  The contradiction proves that in the original \
set-up, no ",
  StyleBox["three ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of the points are collinear.  That was the second step of the proof."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We are almost finished with a proof that ends in a contradiction.  Go back \
to the original dependence relationship armed with all the knowledge we have \
obtained.  Take a non-zero conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (which will have to be non-degenerate) passing through the ",
  StyleBox["first five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p\_1, \ p\_2, p\_3, p\_4, p\_5}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  By assumption, this conic ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " additionally pass through ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the remaining points, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_7\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)   Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a line passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_6\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_7\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  It ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " pass through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?) Define a ",
  StyleBox["cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k == h\ n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If we apply the function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[k, #]&\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to both sides of the original dependence equation, we will annihilate all \
but the last term.  (Why?)  But, it is impossible to have the result equal to \
zero, and we finally have a contradiction!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Can the method of this proof be generalized to higher degrees?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The method of proof does give us other results about ",
  StyleBox["cubics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " If two distinct non-degenerate cubics intersect in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["9",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " points, and if three of the points are ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then the remaining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["6",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["conconic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Let the nine points of intersection be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ... , ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_9\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since they lie on ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " curves, they are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent. We therefore have a non-trivial dependence relationship:\n  \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(a\_1\ p\_1\%3 + a\_2\ p\_2\%3 + a\_3\ p\_3\%3 + a\_4\ p\_4\%3 + 
            a\_5\ p\_5\%3 + a\_6\ p\_6\%3 + a\_7\ p\_7\%3 + a\_8\ p\_8\%3 + 
            a\_9\ p\_9\%3 == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nNone of coefficients can be ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because every subset of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["8",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-independent.  (Why?) Suppose the collinear triple is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["{",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["p",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["1",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]], ",", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["p",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["2",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]], ",", 
          StyleBox[
            SubscriptBox["p", 
              StyleBox["3",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]],
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ", which determine a line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This line does not pass through any of the remaining points.  By \
applying the function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[l, #]&\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to both sides of the equation, we get a dependency equation (without zero \
coefficients) among the squares of the points in \n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["{",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["p",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["4",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]], ",", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["p",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["5",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]], ",", 
          StyleBox[
            SubscriptBox["p", 
              StyleBox["6",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], ",", 
          StyleBox[
            SubscriptBox["p", 
              StyleBox["7",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], ",", 
          StyleBox[
            SubscriptBox["p", 
              StyleBox["8",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], ",", 
          StyleBox[
            SubscriptBox["p", 
              StyleBox["9",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]],
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ".\nHence, if we pass a conic through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["5",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of the points, it must pass through the sixth.  Therefore, all six points \
are conconic. ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Collinear inflections", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The inflection points on a cubic make a very interesting configuration of \
points and lines.  It will take us some time to get all the facts together, \
but we can prove at once a basic property of ",
  StyleBox["collinearity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The line passing through ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection points of a cubic must also pass through a ",
  StyleBox["third",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Let us declare that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " if a (symbolic) cubic and that we have some points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, r, s, t, Subscript]; \nf /: DegP[f] = 3; \n
    p /: DegP[p] = 1; \nq /: DegP[q] = 1; \nr /: DegP[r] = 1; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "First we declare that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are points on the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((p /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^3]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^3]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the intersection of the two tangents to the curve at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2\ r]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^2\ r]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"We want to have inflection points, so all we have to do is declare \
\[LongDash] making use of the comon point on the two tangents \[LongDash] \
that the tangent has a higher degree contact with the curve."], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ r\^2]]; \n
    \((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\ r\^2]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next we introduce a third point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(s = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], q]\)\ p + PD[PD[PD[f, p], p], q]\ q; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since we are thinking of two ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection points, neither can lie on the tangent determined by the \
other, and so neither of the coefficients just used is zero.  Therefore, the \
point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["distinct third point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We check it lies on the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, s\^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It does!  Now we calculate the tangent at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t = PD[f, s\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider this point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["o",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(o = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], q]\)\ r + 2\ PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], r]\ q; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By construction it lies on the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["PD[t,o]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But it cannot ",
  StyleBox["be",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", since it is on the tangent at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (which is the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[q, r]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").  We have only to check that the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["o",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["higher-degree contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, o\ s\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It does!  And that is a proof.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "As an exercise, let us ",
  StyleBox["construct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a cubic with some predetermined inflection points.  Here is the general \
cubic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, a, p, q, r, Subscript]; \n
    f = Table[a\_i, {i, 10}] . Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Let us take some points:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(p = w; q = 2\ u + w; r = u + v + w; \nPD[f, p\^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be on the curve, one coefficient has to be zeroed out."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = f /. a\_10 \[Rule] 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now let us make ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " inflection points as we did in the above proof."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{PD[f, q\^3] == 0, PD[f, \(p\^2\) r] == 0, 
        PD[f, \(q\^2\) r] == 0, PD[f, p\ r\^2] == 0, PD[f, q\ r\^2] == 0}, 
      Table[a\_i, {i, 9}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We then simplify the cubic.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = 
      \((f /. {a\_1 \[Rule] a\_6\/2 + a\_7\/2 + a\_9\/4, 
              a\_2 \[Rule] a\_7\/2 + a\_9\/4, 
              a\_3 \[Rule] \(-\(a\_6\/2\)\) - a\_7\/2 - a\_9\/4, 
              a\_5 \[Rule] \(-a\_6\) - a\_7, a\_8 \[Rule] \(-a\_9\)})\) /. {
          a\_9 \[Rule] 1}\)], "Input"],

Cell["As an example, we specialize the remaining coefficients.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = f /. {a\_4 \[Rule] 1, a\_6 \[Rule] 1, a\_7 \[Rule] 1}\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is a double check:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, p\^3], PD[f, q\^3], PD[f, \(p\^2\) r], PD[f, \(q\^2\) r], 
      PD[f, p\ r\^2], PD[f, q\ r\^2]}\)], "Input"],

Cell["Let us draw a picture now.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 4}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It looks as though we have two predetermined inflections.  What about that \
other point on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{f == 0, y == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Yes, it is on the curve, and here is its tangent:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(PD[f, \((\(-\(\(2\ u\)\/5\)\) + w)\)\^3]\), 
    \(\(PD[f, \((\(-\(\(2\ u\)\/5\)\) + w)\)\^2] // Tidy\) // Factor\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell["Does the tangent have triple intersection with the curve?", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{f == 0, 5\ x + 3\ y + 2\ z == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Yes! The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-\(\(2\ u\)\/5\)\) + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an inflection point."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A cubic with real coefficients can have only ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " real inflection points.  As we will find out, the others (if any) have to \
be ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Hessians and Inflections", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Using the Hessian", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Picturing the Hessian", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Earlier we found a very nice cubic, so let us calculate the Hessian \
and see where it intersects the curve.  (We know those intersections have to \
be inflection points of both curves.)\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pts = {w, u + w, 2\ u + w, v + w, 2\ v + w, u + v + w, 
        PE[PE[u + w, 2\ v + w], PE[2\ u + w, v + w]], u + 2\ v + w, 
        3\ u + v + w}; \)\), 
    \(f = Last[Factor[P3P@@pts]]\), 
    \(h = Last[Factor[Det[MHP[f]]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, since we used originally points with ",
  StyleBox["rational",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coordinates, all the curves and Hessians can be written with ",
  StyleBox["rational",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (even, ",
  StyleBox["integer",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") ",
  StyleBox["coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Here is a picture of the two curves and the nine points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ch = 
      ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-3\), 4}, {y, \(-6\), 3}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 50, 
        AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
        PlotStyle 
          \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}}, 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity]; \n\(dd = Draw[pts]; \)\), 
    \(\(Show[{ch, dd}, DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "According to the theorem we proved, the Hessian is supposed to cut the \
curve ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the inflection points.  The picture looks quite convincing.  We can \
try solving for the inflection points, but, in this case, the exact solutions \
are ",
  StyleBox["complicated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " even though the two curves were fairly simple.  For the moment we take \
just numerical solutions."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sol = NSolve[{f == 0, h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, y}]; \)\), 
    \(TableForm[sol]\), 
    \(Length[sol]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We get the required ",
  StyleBox["nine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points, three of which are ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  They should also be ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ip = 
      x\ u + y\ v + w /. 
        sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]{1, 2, 7}\[RightDoubleBracket]; \)\), 
    \(TableForm[ip]\), 
    \(Det[MP[ip]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Close enough to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "!  Let us see how the ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the three points fit into the figure."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, h == 0}, \((PD[f, #\^2] == 0&)\)/@ip] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, 
        \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-6\), 4}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 50, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \n\t
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], Blue}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Blue}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Drawing in the tangents makes the inflection points look much more like \
inflections.  ",
  StyleBox["But",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we see something else in this picture: ",
  StyleBox[
  "The tangents at the inflection points seem to be tangent to the Hessian!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The point of tangency is at another location, since the Hessian \
definitely crosses the curve at an inflection point and does not share the \
tangent at that point (even though it is also an inflection point of the \
Hessian). "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Is there a theorem here?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us look at the ",
  StyleBox["conic polars",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve at the points.  They are of course degenerate conics \
\[LongDash] since this was one way to ",
  StyleBox["detect",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection points.   There shold be some interesting relationships among \
them."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["These pictures may take a lot of memory to run.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "SmallText",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, h == 0}, \((PD[f, #] == 0&)\)/@ip] /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-6\), 4}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Orange}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["What a great picture!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The points where the inflectional tangents seem to be tangent to the \
Hessian look like the ",
  StyleBox["double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic polars.  The three additional lines coming from these polars \
are ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Moreover, each pair of inflectional tangents ",
  StyleBox["intersect",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the conic polar of the other inflection point.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Is this all a dream ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(= just an ",
  StyleBox["artifact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the computer graphics) ",
  StyleBox["or are these coincidents literally true?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But perhaps there is ",
  StyleBox["even more",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to find out about.  If we look closely, it seems as though any two of the \
double points are collinear with a third inflextion point.  To check this \
conjecture \[LongDash] numerically \[LongDash] we have to first find the \
coordinates of the double points.  We do it by intersecting the tangent at an \
inflection point with the Hessian."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tp1 = 
      NSolve[{PD[f, ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]\^2] == 0, 
            h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, y}]; \)\), 
    \(TableForm[tp1]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Because two of the roots are the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", this indicates that the line is also ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the Hessian (at a point different from the inflection point).  Here \
are the other two solution sets."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tp2 = 
      NSolve[{PD[f, ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\^2] == 0, 
            h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, y}]; \)\), 
    \(TableForm[tp2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tp3 = 
      NSolve[{PD[f, ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket]\^2] == 0, 
            h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, y}]; \)\), 
    \(TableForm[tp3]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Now we can give coordinates to the polar double points.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(dp1 = 
      x\ u + y\ v + w /. tp1\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\), 
    \(dp2 = 
      x\ u + y\ v + w /. tp2\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\), 
    \(dp3 = 
      x\ u + y\ v + w /. tp3\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now we can draw in the three lines joining the double points.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, \n\t\t\tPE[dp1, dp2] == 0, \n\t\t\tPE[dp1, dp3] == 0, \n
            \t\t\tPE[dp2, dp3] == 0, \n\t\t\t
            PE[ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket], 
                ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]] == 0, \n\t\t\t
            h == 0}, \((PD[f, #1] == 0&)\)/@ip] /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-6\), 4}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.005], Black}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Black}, {Thickness[0.005], Black}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Gray}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}, \n
          \t\t\t{Thickness[0.005], Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Orange}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "They ",
  StyleBox["look",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as though they pass through the inflection points.  We can make a \
numerical check of this."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Det[MP[{dp1, dp2, ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket]}]]\), 
    \(Det[MP[{dp1, dp3, ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]}]]\), 
    \(Det[MP[{dp2, dp3, ip\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]}]]\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To within a reasonable degree of accuracy, we can say these quantities are \
",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "These numerical calculations are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a proof of a general fact."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Hessian of the Hessian", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We use equations with a ",
  StyleBox["parameter",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to show different examples of curves."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  We will find here a rather special curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, h, e]; \)\), 
    \(f = Tidy[y\^2\ z - x\ \((x - z)\)\ \((x - e\ z)\)]\), 
    \(h = Tidy[\(-\(1\/8\)\)\ Det[MHP[f]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, h == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] \(-1\)}, {x, 
        \(-2\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 50, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Both the cubic and its Hessian intersect the line at infinity at the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  Here is a check that the point ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an inflection point of both curves."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, v\^3], PD[f, v\^2\ u], PD[f, v\ u\^2], PD[f, v\^2], \n\t
        PD[f, v]} /. e \[Rule] \(-1\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[h, v\^3], PD[h, v\^2\ w], PD[h, v\ w\^2], PD[h, v\^2], \n\t
        PD[h, v]} /. e \[Rule] \(-1\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can then solve algebraically for the other eight inflection \
points.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sol = 
      Solve[{f == 0, h == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] \(-1\)}, {x, y}]; 
    \)\), 
    \(TableForm[sol]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The expressions are not so bad, but they can be \"seen\" somewhat \
better in numerical form.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[N[sol]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are clearly only two ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " solutions in this listing.  We next make a list of ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the (exact) inflection points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(infl = Join[{v}, x\ u + y\ v + w /. sol]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Just to be 100% certain, we can check that all nine points lie on \
the two curves.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Simplify[\((PD[f /. e \[Rule] \(-1\), #1\^3]&)\)/@infl]\), 
    \(Simplify[\((PD[h /. e \[Rule] \(-1\), #1\^3]&)\)/@infl]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["The do!  Let us focus on the last one.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(p = Last[infl]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here is the conic polar (which includes the inflectional tangent) \
at that point.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, PD[f, p] == 0, h == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, 
          e \[Rule] \(-1\)}, \n\t{x, \(-1\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Blue}, \n
          \t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the conic polar to the Hessian at the same point.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, PD[h, p] == 0, h == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, 
          e \[Rule] \(-1\)}, \n\t{x, \(-1\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Green}, \n
          \t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Wait!  It is tangent to the original curve!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  And when we draw them together, we find they fit together too well."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, PD[f, p] == 0, h == 0, PD[h, p] == 0} /. {
          z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] \(-1\)}, \n\t{x, \(-1\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, 
      \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \t
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Blue}, \n
          \t\t\ \ {Thickness[0.007], Red}, \n
          \t\ \ \t{Thickness[0.007], Green}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This seems too good to be true.  The explanation lies in the \
Hessian of the Hessian.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(k = Last[Factor[Det[MHP[h /. e \[Rule] \(-1\)]]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f /. e \[Rule] \(-1\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see it is the ",
  StyleBox["original curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " when the parameter is set to ",
  StyleBox["e == \[Dash]1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  But this is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in general true \[LongDash] even though the Hessian of the Hessian of the \
Hessian . . . of the original curve will pass through the same set of \
inflection points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We go back to the original parametric family and compute ",
  StyleBox["the Hessian of the Hessian",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, h, k, e]; \)\), 
    \(f = Tidy[y\^2\ z - x\ \((x - z)\)\ \((x - e\ z)\)]\), 
    \(h = Tidy[\(-\(1\/8\)\)\ Det[MHP[f]]]\), 
    \(k = Map[Factor, Tidy[\(-\(1\/8\)\)\ Det[MHP[h]]], {2}]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Clearly from the algebra, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["k",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is usually ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the original curve.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a picture when ",
  StyleBox["e == \[Dash]2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, h == 0, k == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, 
          e \[Rule] \(-2\)}, {x, \(-2\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \t\n\t
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}, 
          \n\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Green}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Whatever the value of ",
  StyleBox["e",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", the point ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an inflection point."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, v\^3], PD[h, v\^3], Factor[PD[f, v]], Factor[PD[h, v]]}\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the picture when ",
  StyleBox["e == 3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, h == 0, k == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] 3}, {
        x, \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, \t
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}, 
          \n\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Green}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e == \[Dash]2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we can solve for the inflection points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      Solve[{f == 0, h == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] \(-2\)}, {x, y}]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are ",
  StyleBox["8",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of them, and the ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th and",
  StyleBox[" 7",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th are the pair of finite real ones symmetric about the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Length[sol]\), 
    \(TableForm[N[sol]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We are going to focus on the upper of the two real inflection \
points.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(p = x\ u + y\ v + w /. sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]6\[RightDoubleBracket]; 
    \)\), 
    \(pn = N[p, 20]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, PD[f, pn] == 0, h == 0, PD[h, pn] == 0, 
          k == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] \(-2\)}, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Blue}, 
          \n\t\t\ \ {Thickness[0.007], Red}, \n
          \t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Green}, \n
          \t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Orange}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We see the tangent to the curve at the inflection point is also \
tangent to the Hessian at the double point of the conic polar.  And, by the \
same token, the tangent to the Hessian is also tangent to the Hessian of the \
Hessian.  \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[
  "Is all this true?  (At least the pictures suggest this conclusion.)",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
We will have to give some proofs below.  But, first let us see \
whether we can calculate the that point of tangency on the Hessian.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(q = Last[NullSpace[MHP[PD[f /. e \[Rule] \(-2\), p]]]] . {u, v, w}\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Wow!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " That is somewhat messy, but numerically it seems about right."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[q]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can try to check that ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is indeed the necessary double point."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[PD[f /. e \[Rule] \(-2\), p\ q]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox[
"It works!  Amazing!  Is that symbolic computation or what?  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Unfortunately, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does not seem to simplify the exact algebraic expressions for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " very much.  Is there any way to get better expressions for these results \
involving many radicals?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Inasmuch as the Hessian of a non-singular cubic has to pass through the \
inflection points of the given curve, then as soon as we can prove the \
Hessian is itself non singular and we know there are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["9",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of these points, we can prove from the information already developed:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a non-singular cubic, and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be its Hessian.  \tIn general the Hessian ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is distinct from both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "; however, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(h == a\ f + b\ g\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", for suitable scalars ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Relations among the inflection points", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Proving the double point is on the Hessian", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
From our pictures of examples, we saw the close connection between \
a cubic and its Hessian, once we started drawing tangents at the inflection \
points.  It is time to prove these properties in general.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a ",
  StyleBox["non-singular cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ",
  StyleBox["Hessian",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "be an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then we have:\n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["lies on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ";\n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["ii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[") ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The tangent line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is also ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the Hessian at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "; and\n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["iii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[q, r]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["common factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " We use symbolic computation. ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We first declare that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a cubic and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["hh",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be its standard Hessian."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, hh]; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: DegP[f] = 3; \)\), 
    \(hh = Det[MHP[f]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We single out three special points and ",
  StyleBox["recompute",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the Hessian with resprct to the new basis ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[p, q, r]; \)\), 
    \(\((\((# /: DegP[#] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r}; \n
    h = hh /. {u \[Rule] p, v \[Rule] q, w \[Rule] r}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As long as the three points are ",
  StyleBox["independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we know this is an ",
  StyleBox["equivalent representation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the Hessian.  We next assume ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the curve, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the tangent, and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a double point of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  As we know, this is enough to make ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " an inflection point."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((p /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^3]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2\ q]]; \n
    \((\((q /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ q]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell["The Hessian then simplifies under these assumed assignments.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We now want to choose a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " but on the tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let us see what we might need to know."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[h, p\^2\ r]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To do this means we can declare that the ",
  StyleBox["last factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is the one which is zero."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^2\ r]]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hence, it follows that the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is also on the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[h, p\ r\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We also want ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be the double point of the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let us see what we will need for this additional assumption."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[h, p\ r]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it comes down to ",
  StyleBox["two lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, we can declare that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the ",
  StyleBox["non-tangent line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\((\((r /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ r\^2]]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have now in fact made ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[h, p\ r]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And, it now follows that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the Hessian."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[h, q\^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But it is also true that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[h, p\ q\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We calculate next the tangent to the Hessian at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[h, q\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see it is ",
  StyleBox["equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[h, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["degenerate conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and since the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are the respective double points of these conics, ",
  StyleBox["and",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " since each point also lies on the other conic, it follows that the ",
  StyleBox["line joining them",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lies on the conic also.  We can also argue that conclusion \
symbolically\""
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, p\ \((a\ q + b\ r)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[h, p\ \((a\ q + b\ r)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This completes the proof.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  This theorem proves that not only does the Hessian of a non-singular \
cubic have to pass through all the inflection points, it also has to pass \
through all the ",
  StyleBox["double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic polars of these inflection points.  Moreover, it is tangent \
to the tangents at the inflection points exactly at these double points.  In \
other words, the Hessian is ",
  StyleBox["very highly constrained!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inflectional harmonies and intersecting polars", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Earlier, we generated a picture to illustrate the following rather \
remarkable theorem.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a ",
  StyleBox["non-singular cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "be three ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["inflection points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   Then:\n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[") ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Each ",
  StyleBox["pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of tangents to these points intersects on the ",
  StyleBox["conic polar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the\n\t     third inflection point;  \n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["ii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[") ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The the non-tangent factors of the three conic polars are ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "; and  \n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["iii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[") ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The double point on the tangent and the inflection point ",
  StyleBox["separate\n\t      harmonically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the points of intersection with the other two tangents."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The symbolic computation to give the proof is effective but somewhat \
long.  We declare first that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a (symbolic) cubic and that we have six points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, r, u, v, w, pp, qq, rr]; \nf /: DegP[f] = 3; \n
    \((\((# /: DegP[#] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r, pp, qq, rr}; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, rr}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are going to be independent (not collinear), so to simplify computations \
below, we can suppose we have made a projective transformation from them to \
the three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We keep to the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, rr}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-notation in discussing the curve, because the alphabetical order helps us \
to remember which points are being used for which purposes."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\({p, q, rr} = {u, v, w}; \),
      FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, we declare that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are points on the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((u /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^3]]; \n
    \((\((v /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^3]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["rr",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the intersection of the first two tangents to the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((w /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\^2\ rr]]; \n
    \((\((w /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\^2\ rr]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We need to have inflection points, so we declare that the tangents have a \
",
  StyleBox["higher degree contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with the curve \[LongDash] making use of the comon point on the two \
tangents."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\((w /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, p\ rr\^2]]; \n
    \((\((w /: # = 0)\)&)\)[PD[f, q\ rr\^2]]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As in a previous proof, we introduce the third (distinct) point on the \
line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(r = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], q]\)\ p + PD[PD[PD[f, p], p], q]\ q; 
    \)\), 
    \(PD[f, r\^3]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It indeed lies on the curve (by construction).  We now introduce \
what will prove to be the intersections of the other two tangents.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pp = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], q]\)\ rr + 2\ PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], rr]\ q; 
    \)\), 
    \(\(qq = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, p], p], q]\)\ rr + 2\ PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], rr]\ p; 
    \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the first check.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((PD[f, p\^2\ #]&)\)/@{pp, qq, rr}\), 
    \(\((PD[f, q\^2\ #]&)\)/@{pp, qq, rr}\), 
    \(\((PD[f, r\^2\ #]&)\)/@{pp, qq, rr}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is fine.  Since the three tangents are ",
  StyleBox["not concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", no point can be common to all three, but each point is common to a ",
  StyleBox["pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of tangents.  However, all three points lie on ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the conic polars."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((PD[f, p\ #\^2]&)\)/@{pp, qq, rr}\), 
    \(\((PD[f, q\ #\^2]&)\)/@{pp, qq, rr}\), 
    \(\((PD[f, r\ #\^2]&)\)/@{pp, qq, rr}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we define the points which will be shown to be the ",
  StyleBox["double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the conic polars."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(s\_1 = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], rr]\)\ p + PD[PD[PD[f, p], p], q]\ rr; \n
    s\_2 = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, q], r], pp]\)\ q + PD[PD[PD[f, q], q], r]\ pp; \n
    s\_3 = \(-PD[PD[PD[f, r], p], qq]\)\ r + PD[PD[PD[f, r], r], p]\ qq; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To show that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\ s\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " it is enough to show it annihilates ",
  StyleBox["three independent points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, p\ s\_1\ #]&)\)/@{p, q, rr}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["It does!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Now to check the other points for the same kind of property."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, q\ s\_2\ #]&)\)/@{p, q, rr}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, r\ s\_3\ #]&)\)/@{p, q, rr}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "They check out.  We note, however, we do not ",
  StyleBox["formally",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\ s\_1] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  What we have is this:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, p\ s\_1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But we ",
  StyleBox["know",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " it is ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by a linear-space-dimension argument.  So we have proved that the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\ rr]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the same as the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This seems interesting, but we do not need to know the fact.  Here is \
the situation with the other double points:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[f, q\ s\_2]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[f, r\ s\_3]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These computations show that the lines \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, q], rr]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, p], rr]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, p], p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \nand also ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, q], q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["dependent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", which of course they must be.  Probably this last fact follows from the \
first two just found."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Let us check that on each of the three tangents there are harmonic \
tetrads.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(RP[p, s\_1, rr, qq]\), 
    \(RP[q, s\_2, pp, rr]\), 
    \(RP[r, s\_3, qq, pp]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Whew! It works.  Now we check that each inflection point is ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with a pair of double points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Factor[s\_2]\), 
    \(Factor[PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], rr]\ r - PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], q]\ s\_1]\), 
    \(PD[PE[r, s\_1], s\_2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Factor[s\_2]\), 
    \(Factor[
      s\_3 + PD[PD[PD[f, p], p], q]\^2\ PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], q]\ 
          PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], rr]\ p]\), 
    \(PD[PE[p, s\_2], s\_3]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Factor[s\_1]\), 
    \(Factor[s\_3 + PD[PD[PD[f, p], p], q]\^3\ PD[PD[PD[f, p], q], rr]\ q]
      \), 
    \(PD[PE[q, s\_3], s\_1]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What this means is that the triangles ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({pp, qq, rr}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({s\_1, s\_2, s\_3}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["axially perspective",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the line joining the inflection points.  Hence, the joins of the \
corresponding vertices must be ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by Desargues' Theorem.  Here is a check."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[PE[pp, s\_1], PE[PE[qq, s\_2], PE[rr, s\_3]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And that establishes formally all the \"facts\" we discovered by numerical \
computation in a special case by viewing the picture of the cubic and its \
Hessian.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In connection with what we learned in the proof just given, let us take ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["three collinear points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (not just inflection points) on the cubic and look at the corresponding ",
  
  StyleBox["conic polars",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  For example, taking the cubic we used before, we of course know that \
the three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({w, u + w, 2\ u + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " form a collinear triple.  Here is a picture of the conic polars at those \
points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(pts = {w, u + w, 2\ u + w, v + w, 2\ v + w, u + v + w, \n\t\t
        PE[PE[u + w, 2\ v + w], PE[2\ u + w, v + w]], u + 2\ v + w, 
        3\ u + v + w}; \n\(f = Last[Factor[P3P@@pts]]; \)\), 
    \(h = Last[Factor[Det[MHP[f]]]]; \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, h == 0}, \((PD[f, #1] == 0&)\)/@{w, u + w, 2\ u + w}] /. 
        z \[Rule] 1, {x, \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-6\), 4}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}, 
          \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.005], Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Orange}}]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and the cubic curve ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " share the ",
  StyleBox["same tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", namely the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^2]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  So the two curves are ",
  StyleBox["tangent to each other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This is perhaps not so obvious in this picture for this particular \
choice of points.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "What is obvious, however, is that the ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "conic polars ",
  StyleBox["intersect a lot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Why is this?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Well, the three points  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p\_1, p\_2, p\_3}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  are collinear and all are on the cubic.  Thus, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{" ", 
        StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " is a linear combination of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{" ", 
        StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Hence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\_3]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has to be a linear combination of the polars ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  So, they form three conics in a ",
  StyleBox["pencil of conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The first two intersect in four points, and so the the third has to \
pass through the ",
  StyleBox["same four points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (By the way, this argument by linear combinations does not even require \
that the three given points lie on the cubic.)  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, in the case of the inflection points on the cubic, the conic polars \
are ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But they are still conics and this linear combination argument still \
applies:  They all belong to the same pencil.  This observation explains why \
we have the four points of intersection lines in the figure we considered \
earlier."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sol = NSolve[{f == 0, h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, y}]; \)\), 
    \(ip = 
      x\ u + y\ v + w /. 
        sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]{1, 2, 7}\[RightDoubleBracket]; \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, h == 0}, \((PD[f, #1] == 0&)\)/@ip] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, 
        \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-6\), 4}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.007], Red}, 
          \n\t\t\t{Thickness[0.005], Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.005], Orange}}]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But this ",
  StyleBox["does not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " explain why the double points lie on the Hessian, and we had to give \
another proof for that."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"X 3.0",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1280}, {0, 1024}},
ScreenStyleEnvironment->"Working",
WindowSize->{798, 562},
WindowMargins->{{Automatic, 117}, {Automatic, 89}},
Magnification->1,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]


